/*****************************************************************************
    Open questions
*****************************************************************************/
    1  ANSWERED (Tony, 2026-11-12): no. The 11-07 ruling covers it: a run ends at its closing mark or at the paragraph's end, whichever comes first.
    2  The pour's floor request: Clay's draft (text, font, size, colour, box, inset in; fit and used written back) is to be confirmed when the stroke opens.
    3  The pour cursor and break record: one Buffer mark is enough for one flat buffer poured in order, and not for a tree of runs, re-flow on resize, or a frame read twice. Proposal: a cursor (run, offset) plus a break record kept out of the source tree.
    4  Whitespace-only text carries no data in kant, so spaces between runs must be glue, never string runs.
    5  A failed parse abandons the rest of the input at exit 0; for a document that is a silently lost chapter, so the text mode must fail loudly or carry an end sentinel.
    6  Checks owed when the stroke opens: who calls getText today and whether any caller relied on its following a held group; whether a held group holds another group in practice.

